Presidential elections were held in [[Mongolia]] on 26 June 2017. Incumbent [[President of Mongolia|president]] [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]], first elected in [[2009 Mongolian presidential election|2009]] and re-elected in [[2013 Mongolian presidential election|2013]], was constitutionally barred from running for a third term.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20170212091458/http://en.montsame.mn/politics/8495 Presidential election planned for June 26] [[Montsame]], 26 January 2017</ref> 

For the first time, no candidate received a majority vote in the first round, forcing a run-off between the [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] (DP) candidate [[Khaltmaagiin Battulga]] and the [[Mongolian People's Party]] (MPP) candidate [[Miyeegombyn Enkhbold]] on 7 July, brought forward from 9 July.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-mongolia-elections-idUSKBN19H238 No decisive winner in Mongolia presidential vote: election committee] Reuters, 26 June 2017</ref><ref>[http://montsame.mn/en/read/10648 Presidential election:second ballot on July 9] [[Montsame]], 26 June 2017</ref><ref>[http://asiarussia.ru/news/16815/ Election of the President of Mongolia moved to July 7] ARD, 28 June 2017 {{in lang|mn}}</ref> The third-placed [[Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party (2010)|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] (MPRP) candidate [[Sainkhuugiin Ganbaatar]] refused to recognise the results after he missed out on the second round, due to finishing 1,849 votes behind Enkhbold. Ganbaatar claimed fraud and that an additional 35,000 votes had been added to the total. The MPRP demanded a recount of votes in [[Bayan-Ölgii Province]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Началось. Ганбаатар: Мы не признаем сообщения ЦИК. ВИДЕО|url=http://asiarussia.ru/news/16777/|publisher=ARD|date=27 June 2017|accessdate=27 June 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170630072902/http://asiarussia.ru/news/16777/|archive-date=30 June 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In the second round, Battulga was narrowly elected with 50.61% of the valid votes, or 55% of the votes cast for a candidate, while Enkhbold received 44.85% of the votes cast. Battulga was officially inaugurated as the fifth president of Mongolia on 10 July 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=2017-07-10 |title=Inauguration address by Khaltmaagiin Battulga, the president of Mongolia, at the ceremony of presidential swearing into office |url=https://president.mn/en/2017/07/10/inauguration-address-by-khaltmaagiin-battulga-the-president-of-mongolia-at-the-ceremony-of-presidential-swearing-into-office/ |access-date=2025-08-30 |website=[[President of Mongolia]] |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== Background ==

=== 2016 parliamentary election ===
{{Further|2016 Mongolian parliamentary election}}
In the [[2016 Mongolian parliamentary election|2016 parliamentary election]], the then-governing [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] (DP) faced a major electoral defeat, maintaining only 9 of its previous 35 seats in the [[State Great Khural]]. The MPP, in comparison, won a [[supermajority]] of 65 seats. Alongside the two dominant parties, the MPRP and an [[Independent politician|independent]] candidate, respectively, won a single seat.<ref>{{cite news |date=30 June 2016 |title=Mongolian opposition wins landslide, voters fed up with hard times |url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-mongolia-election-idUKKCN0ZF28C |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160817034324/http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-mongolia-election-idUKKCN0ZF28C |archive-date=17 August 2016 |access-date=27 August 2016 |newspaper=Reuters}}</ref>

Despite the landslide victory, Enkhbold, as [[Chairman of the Mongolian People's Party|chairperson of the MPP]], decided not to assume the role of [[Prime Minister of Mongolia|prime minister]] and instead became [[Chairman of the State Great Khural|speaker of parliament]]. [[Jargaltulgyn Erdenebat]] was nominated and appointed as the next prime minister, which became a setback for Enkhbold's personal popularity.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Tserenjamts |first=Munkhtsetseg |date=2017 |title=Digital Populism and the Social Media Impact on the 2017 Mongolian Presidential Election |url=https://www.kas.de/documents/288143/288192/7_file_storage_file_26301_2.pdf/78c71b78-0ab8-7aaf-7122-5a91afc548e4 |access-date=2026-02-17 |website=[[Konrad Adenauer Foundation]] |pages=2-6 |language=en}}</ref> The 2016 local elections in October saw the MPP win another landslide victory in all of the [[Provinces of Mongolia|provincial]], [[Districts of Mongolia|district]], and [[Ulaanbaatar|capital]] [[Governor|governorships]] and legislatures, all except [[Zavkhan Province]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mendee |first= |date=2017-05-31 |title=Presidential Election: A Game Already Started |url=https://blogs.ubc.ca/mongolia/2017/presidential-election-a-game-already-started/ |access-date=2026-02-16 |website=Mongolia Focus |language=en-US}}</ref>

Prior to the 2017 presidential election, the Erdenebat cabinet's decisions to increase seven types of taxes, cut social care, and extend the retirement age all faced public outcry. A range of public opinion surveys conducted before or during the election established that these decisions led to a disillusionment with the MPP.<ref name=":4" />

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Mongolia]] is elected using the [[two-round system]].<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2277/ Mongolia] IFES</ref> Mongolian electoral law considers the blank votes casts in presidential elections as valid votes. The [[General Election Commission of Mongolia|General Election Commission]] (GEC) thus includes blank votes in its calculations of the proportion of the vote won by each candidate; as a result, it is possible for no candidate to receive a majority of the vote in the second round. If this happens, the entire election is annulled and fresh elections would be held with new candidates.<ref>[http://aceproject.org/ero-en/regions/asia/MN/mongolia-electoral-law-for-state-great-hural-and/at_download/file Law on Election] Ace Project</ref>

==Nominees==
Three political parties with seats in the [[State Great Khural]] were eligible to nominate a presidential candidate; the [[Mongolian People's Party]] (MPP), the [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] (DP) and the [[Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party (2010)|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] (MPRP).<ref name="UBP">[https://web.archive.org/web/20170116170218/http://theubpost.mn/2017/01/09/6509/ Who will be the fifth President of Mongolia?] The UB Post, 9 January 2017</ref>

=== Mongolian People's Party ===
Candidates for the MPP presidential bid were selected from a party public survey on 2 May 2017. About 50,000 people participated in the sociological survey. The top five most favoured politicians — [[Miyeegombyn Enkhbold]], [[Tsendiin Nyamdorj]], [[Ölziisaikhany Enkhtüvshin]], [[Ukhnaagiin Khürelsükh]], [[Badmaanyambuugiin Bat-Erdene]] — were proposed and discussed as potential primary candidates. During the party board meeting on 2 May, Enkhtüvshin, Khürelsükh and Bat-Erdene withdrew their names to support the party policy.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Т |first=Өлзийбаяр |date=2017-05-03 |title=М.Энхболд, Ц.Нямдорж хоёрын хэнийг нь Ерөнхийлөгчид нэр дэвшүүлэхийг шийднэ |url=https://ikon.mn/n/101o |access-date=2026-02-16 |website=ikon.mn |language=mn}}</ref>

Enkhbold and Nyamdorj ran for the MPP presidential nomination on 3 May 2017, during a party conference. Out of the 259 governing board members, Enkhbold received 85% of the total vote and was chosen as the MPP pick for the 2017 presidential election.<ref name=":1" /> Nyamdorj was eliminated from the primary with 14.3%<ref>{{Cite web |last=Т |first=Өлзийбаяр |date=2017-05-03 |title=МАН-аас М.Энхболд ерөнхийлөгчийн сонгуульд нэр дэвшихээр боллоо |url=https://ikon.mn/n/101x |access-date=2026-02-16 |website=ikon.mn |language=mn}}</ref>
